# Store every text file with LF, and check it out with LF too, overriding
# core.autocrlf on Windows.
#
# The second half of that is the point. Git already normalises to LF on commit,
# so the repository was never the problem; the working copy was. This tree was
# written on Windows, where anything opening a file in text mode turns "\n" into
# CRLF on the way out, which left some files CRLF and others LF.
#
# That is not merely untidy. A pattern anchored with "foo$" does not match a
# line ending "foo\r", so a search-and-replace across the tree silently skipped
# the CRLF files - and the grep written to verify it skipped exactly the same
# ones, for exactly the same reason, and reported success. Pinning the checkout
# to LF removes the whole class.
#
# ESP-IDF builds on Linux and macOS as well, so LF is the right stored form
# regardless.
* text=auto eol=lf
